  "full_response": "Eventual consistency is a consistency model used in distributed systems, which ensures that, given enough time without any new updates, all nodes in the system will agree on the same value. In other words, if a write operation is on one node, the system will eventually, but not immediately, ensure that this change is visible to all other nodes.\n\n\nDue to the inherent nature of eventual consistency, there is a trade-off between consistency and availability. Systems that employ eventual consistency will prioritize making the system available over having it give you consistent data at all times. There can be multiple versions of a data item (e.g., timestamps), and these versions are reconciled over time through specific processes, such as conflict resolution.\n\n\nEventual consistency is suitable for applications where immediate consistency is not necessary and where network partitions are expected. Typical examples include content delivery networks (CDNs) and some types of cache systems.\n\n\nIn summary, eventual consistency is a model that guarantees that, given the opportunity, every read operation will receive the most recent write observed by some node in the distributed system. This model has been adopted by many modern distributed systems where trade-offs between availability and consistency, as described by the CAP theorem are acceptable.",
